# Fast loading

Every route and feature must feel instant. Cheaper Firebase usage (see **firebase-cost**) and fast UX go together — fewer listeners and smaller chunks both help.

## First paint

- Show shell + skeleton immediately; never `return null` while auth or data loads (`LayoutGate`, `LoadingState`, route-level skeletons).
- Code-split heavy pages and below-fold blocks with `next/dynamic` + a skeleton that matches final layout.
- Defer non-critical UI (admin widgets, dialogs, secondary panels) until after the primary content paints.
- No staggered entrance animations on lists above the fold; honour `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Data

- Gate realtime listeners by route; unsubscribe on leave.
- Prefer cache-first reads where staleness is acceptable.
- Bound queries; paginate or load-on-open — never unbounded scans on mount.
- One shared provider per dataset, not per-row listeners.

## Assets

- Compress and resize before Storage upload; thumbnails in lists, originals on open.
- Do not prefetch full images, PDFs, or large files the user has not requested.
- Reuse URLs on documents; avoid `getDownloadURL` every render.

## Bundles

- Import only what the route needs; lazy-load rare modals and admin tools.
- Keep homepage and hub entry paths lean — split large components by section when each section owns its hooks.

If a change adds blocking work, extra listeners, or large client bundles, choose the faster design or explain the trade-off.
